Inhomogeneous direct correlation function from MD via OZ inversion (proof of concept)
=====================================================================================

These examples drive "compute structure/factor/2d", which measures the bin-resolved
(planar) static structure factor S_ij(k_parallel) of a fluid whose density varies
along z.  Time-averaged with fix ave/time, S_ij(k) is inverted (in Python) through the
inhomogeneous Ornstein-Zernike equation to obtain the inhomogeneous direct correlation
function C(z1,z2,s).  For a liquid-vapor slab this yields the Triezenberg-Zwanzig (TZ)
inhomogeneous Helmholtz energy density psi_IH(z) and surface tension directly from MD,
instead of the local-density + Percus-Yevick approximation.

Requirements
------------
- LAMMPS built with the EXTRA-COMPUTE package.
- Python 3 with NumPy (matplotlib optional, for --plot).
- The simulation box must be square in x and y (Lx == Ly); the compute aborts otherwise.

compute structure/factor/2d syntax
----------------------------------
    compute ID group-ID structure/factor/2d kmax nbins

    kmax  = maximum reciprocal-lattice index (circular cutoff radius in k-space)
    nbins = number of z-bins (interface-normal direction)

Output: a global array with columns [q=|k_parallel|, ibin, jbin, S_ij, density].
The per-bin density rho_i is stored on the q==0 rows (column 5).  Values are
instantaneous and unnormalized -- divide by area and time-average externally.

Workflow
--------
1. Bulk validation:
       lmp -in in.bulk_lj
       python3 oz_invert.py --mode bulk --sf-file sf_bulk.out --nbins 8 \
               --lx <Lx> --lz <Lz> --temp 0.90 --rdf-file rdf_bulk.out
   Checks that the inverted C_ij is translationally invariant in z and that its
   z-Fourier transform reproduces the bulk c(k) ~ (1 - 1/S(q))/rho.

2. Liquid-vapor slab:
       lmp -in in.slab_lj
       python3 oz_invert.py --mode slab --sf-file sf_slab.out --nbins 40 \
               --lx <Lx> --lz <Lz> --temp 0.80
   Produces psi_IH(z) and the TZ surface tension; compare gamma to the mechanical
   value in gamma_slab.out.

Get Lx and Lz from the LAMMPS log (printed box bounds) or thermo output.

Notes / caveats
---------------
- The OZ inversion is ill-conditioned in low-density (vapor) bins; oz_invert.py drops
  bins below --rho-min and reports the condition number of (I + D h).
- The smallest accessible wavevector is k = 2*pi/Lx, which limits the small-k fit used
  for the TZ second moment.  For Lx ~ 13 sigma this is a SYSTEMATIC LOW BIAS of order
  2x in gamma_TZ (the k^2 regime is not reached; J0(ks) corrections matter for the
  s^3-weighted tail of c).  A quadratic fit in k^2 (--fit-order 2) partially corrects
  it; the real remedy is a larger in-plane box.  Validate against the full-cell
  mechanical gamma = Lz*<Pzz-(Pxx+Pyy)/2> (NOT the per-interface value).
- The s^3 weighting in the TZ second moment emphasizes the large-s tail of C(s); a
  -beta*u(s) tail correction beyond s = L/2 is a natural refinement (not yet applied).
- This is a proof of concept: parameters (run length, kmax, nbins, box size) are modest
  and should be converged for production use.
